References: <5abc11d80801260453x52166bf7rfa11754a33d7128@mail.gmail.com> <Pine.LNX.4.64.0801260757390.24244@w5.hmdc.harvard.edu> <5abc11d80801260525q1d2126bbs3564ce7e34d6b89c@mail.gmail.com> <Pine.LNX.4.64.0801260832290.24244@w5.hmdc.harvard.edu> <5abc11d80801260647m10f44635v5994257b4062ace8@mail.gmail.com> <470542488-1201365356-cardhu_decombobulator_blackberry.rim.net-612502770-@bxe022.bisx.prod.on.blackberry> <5abc11d80801270053g67060fd4x218d54b708a4fe6a@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.64.0801271232090.18850@w5.hmdc.harvard.edu>


Often, estimates based on matched data is what you want.  Without 
matching, analyses can be highly 'model dependent', which means that your 
results can very sensitive to minor specification decisions (which can 
make the results you choose to present highly suspect).  If the goal is 
causal inference, preprocessing your data via matching can eliminate much 
of this model dependence.  Have a look at one of the first two papers at 
this web site on model dependence and the third on matching:

 	http://gking.harvard.edu/projects/cause.shtml

Gary


