From gregor.gorjanc-9T0mJvD/FdoBkiSCaE/Esw at public.gmane.org  Tue Jan 29 14:23:15 2008
From: gregor.gorjanc-9T0mJvD/FdoBkiSCaE/Esw at public.gmane.org (Gregor Gorjanc)
Date: Tue, 29 Jan 2008 14:23:15 +0000
Subject: Re: Predicted values for lm.mixed + patches
References: <C53BDCB1-7670-4248-9ABB-37977381C90E@wustl.edu>
Message-ID: <loom.20080129T140236-577@post.gmane.org>

Hello again!

I was bitten today by Zelig ls.mixed model, since due to the nature 
(collinearity) of my data one coefficient (silently!) drops out from the model
fit. The dropout is done by lmer(), but Zelig's sim() can not work with such an 
object.

qi.lmer is using the following code to get the coefficient names:

fTermsNames <- colnames(model.matrix(parsefml$fixed, data = D))

However, in my case above code gives one coefficient to many. I believe that
the following code is:
- more generic
- simpler
- more efficient as there is no need to set up the model.matrix and fixed 
  effects coefficient names are already in simpar or betas objects availale
  in qi.lmer.

fTermsNames <- dimnames(betas)[[2]]

As previously I upload the changed qi.mixed.R to

http://gregor.gorjanc.googlepages.com/qi.mixed.R

I tested (R CMD check with 2.6.1) the package with modfified version of 
qi.mixed and there is not problems. Btw. codetools says "qi.survreg: no 
visible binding for global variable ‘arr.ind’".

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

Very similar code as above is also used for random effects, but I was not able 
to fully understand it and I did not dare to touch it.


Regards, Gregor

